to-gasper-azman-source : List Word
to-gasper-azman-source = lines-of (cons "# Gašper Ažman"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Gašper, this is yours in the graph, and it opens with credit. Hyperactivity's lineage runs through the protocol language you and Avik Jain built at Citadel Securities: a description of an arbitrary wire protocol from which reactive, template-only C++ parser libraries were generated, then optimized further by the compiler around whatever hooks a user wired in, meant to reach NIC and FPGA targets. The paper tells that lineage with you in it"
  (cons (cite metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-source "(Part III, stateboxes)")
  (cons ". Avik's words: “I care more about crediting gasper than anything else.” You hold full credit for every piece of your work the system builds on."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What your work saw that the field undervalues"
  (cons ""
  (cons "C++ treats your work as a set of conveniences. Its weight is in what it removes."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Deducing this** (P0847, with Sy Brand, Ben Deane and Barry Revzin; in C++23) is usually explained as a way to stop writing four overloads. What it does is remove a privileged primitive. Before it, the object a member function acts on was a hidden parameter with special rules; after it, `this` is an ordinary parameter whose type and value category are deduced like any other. The language got smaller, and one definition covers every qualified form of its object. That is a reduction of ontology."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The ordering customization points** in C++20 and **using enum** follow the same line: an existing structure made available through one well-defined interface instead of reimplemented at every use. Your committee work on **contracts** for C++26 extends it to meaning: a precondition stated once in the interface, not an assertion scattered through bodies."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The protocol language** was this view applied at once. A protocol is a specification and the parser is its meaning, run. Generating the parser in template-only C++ means evaluating the specification at compile time, so nothing of the general machinery survives into the bytes that run against the wire: partial evaluation in Futamura's sense, done inside the most demanding staging language in production use. Your work cutting sender/receiver build cost from quadratic to linear is the same discipline turned on the compiler itself."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up from it"
  (cons ""
  (cons (cite to-everyone-source "Hyperactivity, in Avik's words,") (cons "is Wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon, with cubical type theory at its core"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". The paper names the substance your protocol language shares with it, a protocol as a state machine and a message as a firing"
  (cons (cite metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-source "(Part III)")
  (cons ": a handler supplies the interpretation of requests in a particular world"
  (cons (cite s10-p6 "(§10)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**One definition, its variants related by paths.** Deducing this replaces four copies with one definition. In the paper an established equivalence of representations induces a path along which dependent constructions are transported, operations and their laws together"
  (cons (cite s4-p2 "(§4)")
  (cons ". Variants are not kept in sync by discipline; their relation is part of the program."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Specialization that carries its own justification.** The protocol language trusted its generator. Hyperactivity's specializer returns a new code together with a code for its justification"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ", and rewriting a residual by a theorem carries a path checked against the original"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p11 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". When a protocol's header changes, the old decoder proof does not silently become a proof about the new header; the residual parser is recomputed and the new executable artifact constructed"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Contracts as types.** In the paper a requirement on a function is a chosen dependent family, and the reasoning that establishes it is a term over every argument"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons "; correctness is relative to the chosen requirement, not a verdict on the function in isolation"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1)")
  (cons ". The proof attaches to a declaration with no change to runtime behaviour"
  (cons (cite s5-1-p28 "(§5.1)")
  (cons ". A C++26 contract states a precondition and checks it under the chosen evaluation semantics; a dependent type states the same thing and it is discharged for every call."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**C++ inside the host.** A lifted C++ program keeps the distinctions its own standard makes, among permitted results, unspecified choices and undefined behaviour"
  (cons (cite s8-p2 "(§8)")
  (cons ", and a complete presentation accounts for name lookup, overload resolution, templates and instantiation, constant evaluation and object lifetimes"
  (cons (cite s8-p4 "(§8)")
  (cons ". The committee's object of study is a value of the language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this page is in the room"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik's background, and the place this work started, is low-latency market-data stream processing, and the protocol language was where he learned how a staged language meets the hardware, working beside you. You are one of very few people who think about both ends: what the type system can guarantee, and what the machine executes at the wire. The intent is to bring everyone named in this document into one shared conversation. Your piece is the one closest to the machine."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- P0847R7, Deducing this (Ažman, Brand, Deane, Revzin), https://open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2021/p0847r7.html"
  (cons "- CppCast guest page, Gašper Ažman (committee member since 2017; using enum, deducing this, ordering customization points; contracts for C++26; C++: The Beast is Back with Jon Kalb), https://cppcast.com/guests/gasper_azman/"
  (cons "- CppCast episode 325, Deducing This, https://old.cppcast.com/guest/gazman/"
  (cons "- Citadel Securities, Why C++26 Feels Like the Next Major Shift in Systems Programming (sender/receiver build cost from quadratic to linear), https://www.citadelsecurities.com/careers/career-perspectives/why-cpp26-feels-like-the-next-major-shift-in-systems-programming/"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Parts I and III, the definitions linked above"
